    "topic_path": "Root/ACT 4/Scene 5/Enter Laertes King",
    "content": "The doors are broke. Enter Laertes with others. Where is this king?—Sirs, stand you all without. No, let’s come in! I pray you, give me leave. We will, we will. I thank you. Keep the door. Followers exit. O, thou\n vile king,\n Give me my father! Calmly, good Laertes. That drop of blood that’s calm proclaims me\n bastard,\n Cries “cuckold” to my father, brands the harlot\n Even here between the chaste unsmirchèd brow\n Of my true mother. What is the cause, Laertes,\n That thy rebellion looks so giant-like?—\n Let him go, Gertrude. Do not fear our person. There’s such divinity doth hedge a king\n That treason can but peep to what it would,\n Acts little of his will.—Tell me, Laertes,\n Why thou art thus incensed.—Let him go,\n Gertrude.—\n Speak, man.",
